{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792888624,"nonce":"wpbIroyEA9RrP4l5MpTXqJtJKPo=","title":"A styled control makes its transitions, tweens and signals when it mounts, so a Ghost button costs about 25 finalizable handles","message":"**Measured (2026-10-25)**\n1,000 `\u003cbutton variant={Ghost}\u003eRun\u003c/button\u003e` in one tree on Ash (probe: a Div with a `\u003cfor\u003e` of buttons, 3 settle passes, `hl.Gc.dumpMemory` after two majors), typed counts per button: 8 Tweened, 10 Transition, 4 Watch, 7 SignalValue, 4 SignalBool, 5 Color, 2 Owner, 2 Identity, 1 Node, about 37 String and 20 array\u003cdynamic\u003e. A bare `\u003cdiv\u003e` has none of the first six.\nThe census puts the live pointer heap at about 15 KB per button, and the external pressure charge at about 100 KB per button (102 MB for 1,000, against 0.2 MB for 1,000 divs): each finalizable handle charges the collector 4 KiB, and a Tweened, a Transition, a Watch and a signal each hold one. `hl.Gc.stats().currentMemory` reports 70 to 110 KB per button for that reason, which is what ChimpMonk (chimpmonk-6c) measures. `footprint` of the process grew by roughly 60 to 150 KB per button across counts (native layout nodes and text shaping included), so part of it is real.\n\n**Cause**\nThe cascade creates a Transition and a Tweened for each property a rule transitions (the UA sheet gives every control a transition list), when the element is styled, whether or not the property ever changes.\n\n**Change**\nMake them lazy as Interaction's signals were (33de4ec): keep the target value as the truth and make the Tweened and Transition on the first change of that property after the first style pass. Nothing is animated on mount in either case. Check with a probe of 1,000 buttons that the counts fall, that a hover still transitions, and that tests/run.sh, render and the transition fixtures pass.\n\n**Also**\nRayDE and the editor already save memory by not mounting what is not shown: CodeEditor and CodeDiff pool their rows, Select takes `virtualRowHeight`, and RayDE's explorer builds tree items on demand. There is no shared virtual-list component yet.","files":null}]}